<p><a class="cimage" href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/DSC09312.jpg"><img decoding="async"  src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/DSC09312-scaled.jpg" alt="" width="840" height="560" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-125629" srcset="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/DSC09312-scaled.jpg 2560w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/DSC09312-300x200.jpg 300w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/DSC09312-1024x683.jpg 1024w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/DSC09312-768x512.jpg 768w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/DSC09312-1536x1024.jpg 1536w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/DSC09312-2048x1365.jpg 2048w" sizes="(max-width: 840px) 100vw, 840px" /></a></p>
<p>After <a shot href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/2026/11/07/whos-that-actor-revisited/">last night&#8217;s post</a>, I realised I was probably just getting too invested in somehow using imdb for the &#8220;who&#8217;s that actor, anyway?&#8221; command for <a href="https://github.com/larsmagne/movie.el">my Emacs-based video player</a>.</p>
<p>Not only is it an extremely fragile setup that&#8217;s gonna stop working, but it&#8217;s also very slow &#8212; it takes half a minute to return the actor profile images.</p>
<p>So I just implemented a <a href="https://developer.themoviedb.org/docs/getting-started" data-cached-time="2026-11-08T12:44:10" data-cached-image="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/cache-2026-11-08-web.webp">tmdb API shim</a> and then I ask it for the actor profile photo instead.  tmdb doesn&#8217;t have as large a data base, but&#8230;  The API is free (for non-commercial use) and is <a href="https://github.com/larsmagne/movie.el/blob/master/tmdb.el">extremely easy to use</a>.</p>
<p>Things are now faster:</p>
<p><video autoplay loop muted poster="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/posterQ0GgZC001.jpg"><source src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/video-yvUOqX.mp4" type="video/mp4"></video></p>
<p>The main wait is from the response from the LLM &#8212; I&#8217;m using gemini-3.8-flash.  I&#8217;ve tried gemini-3.1-flash-lite, and it&#8217;s much faster&#8230;  but gives the wrong result most of the time.  No, that&#8217;s not Roy Scheider, Gemini!</p>
<p>So now the setup is less Rube Goldberguesque.  Sort of:</p>
<ol>
<li>I hit a key on my li&#8217;l wireless keyboard.
<li>It&#8217;s picked up by a Python script that listens for <tt>evdev</tt> events.
<li>It uses <tt>emacsclient</tt> to notify the Emacs running on the TV machine.
<li>Emacs there tells <tt>mpv</tt> to do a screenshot.
<li>Emacs waits until the screenshot arrives on disc.
<li>Emacs sends it to Gemini and says &#8220;who dis? JSON please&#8221;.
<li>Gemini responds and Emacs then creates an SVG overlay with the actor name.
<li><tt>rsvg-convert</tt> converts to PNG and then <tt>imagemagick</tt> convert to BGRA.
<li>Emacs asks <tt>mpv</tt> to display it.
<li>Emacs asks tmdb for the actor profile URL and fetches the image.
<li>If it gets a response, it then recreates the <tt>svg</tt> with the image embedded.
<li>(repeat 8-9).
</ol>
<p>Couldn&#8217;t be simpler.</p>
